{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radiative View Factor.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Radiative View Factor.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"radiative view factor radiative view factor","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Radiative View Factor.md","fileName":"Radiative View Factor.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Radiative View Factor","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-14 00:55\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Conduction Convection and Radiation]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Conduction Convection and Radiation","Surface-to-Surface Radiation","cosmolheattransfermodels.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radiative View Factor.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Radiative View Factor.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"radiative view factor radiative view factor","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Radiative View Factor.md","fileName":"Radiative View Factor.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Radiative View Factor","heading":"Radiative View Factor","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A radiative view factor is the fraction of energy leaving one surface that reaches another. It is determined by surface area, orientation, separation, visibility, and shadowing rather than by the surfaces' temperatures or material properties.\n\nConvex exterior surfaces cannot see one another in the same way that concave interior surfaces can. [[Surface-to-Surface Radiation|radiation models]] integrate angular contributions over all mutually visible surfaces so that geometric exposure and occlusion are represented in the energy exchange.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Conduction Convection and Radiation","Surface-to-Surface Radiation","cosmolheattransfermodels.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radiative View Factor.